DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The floating-point environment includes exception flags and masks, the
     current rounding mode, and other architecture-specific settings.  How‐
     ever, it does not include the floating-point register file.

     The ffeeggeetteennvv() function stores the current floating-point environment in
     the object pointed to by _e_n_v_p, whereas ffeehhoollddeexxcceepptt() saves the current
     environment, then clears all exception flags and masks all floating-point
     exceptions.

     The ffeesseetteennvv() function restores a previously saved environment.  The
     ffeeuuppddaatteeeennvv() function restores a saved environment as well, but it also
     raises any exceptions that were set in the environment it replaces.

     The ffeehhoollddeexxcceepptt() function is often used with ffeeuuppddaatteeeennvv() or
     ffeesseetteennvv() to suppress spurious exceptions that occur as a result of in‐
     termediate computations.  An example in fenv(3) demonstrates how to do
     this.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The ffeeggeetteennvv(), ffeehhoollddeexxcceepptt(), ffeesseetteennvv(), and ffeeuuppddaatteeeennvv() functions
     return 0 if they succeed, and non-zero otherwise.

